---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: a2o-governance
covers: subtree
purpose: >
  The a2o (acceptance-to-outcome) surface — executable BDD scenarios, Cucumber step definitions,
  script infrastructure for reporting, and the framework runtime. Feature-story writes carry a
  context-blind reader review obligation so their human value and causal narrative stand without
  session context. New feature subdirectories must also carry their own .epr-meta to prevent silent
  governance drop.
rules:
  - id: a2o-story-blind-reader-review
    class: inject
    when: { write: "*.feature" }
    route-to: { dest: blind-reader }
    parameters: { review-profile: a2o-story }
    why: >
      After the authoring pass, dispatch a fresh-context blind-reader with ONLY the completed
      feature-file path and the `a2o-story` review profile. Do not provide the conversation, plan,
      implementation diff, incident history, or related docs. Revise from its interpretability and
      coherence findings, then repeat with a new blind reader until the story is READY or the operator
      explicitly defers named findings. The PostToolUse signal makes this deferred review obligation
      visible after writes; it is one review loop per completed authoring pass, not one subagent
      dispatch per edit.
  - id: new-feature-subdir-needs-meta
    class: dispatch
    when: { write: ".epr-meta", new: true }
    require-sibling: ".epr-meta"
    parameters:
      dispatch-agent: librarian
      dispatch-prompt: >
        Review the new a2o subtree boundary and say whether it needs its own .epr-meta or inherits
        an adequate nearest ancestor. Cite one existing sibling as precedent. Report only; do not
        author governance or interrupt the current development pass.
    why: >
      A new subdirectory under genesis/a2o/ must be born with its own .epr-meta —
      place one alongside the first file so governance is never silently dropped by
      descending one directory. See genesis/a2o/features/dataplane/resiliency-saga/.epr-meta as an example.
  - id: run-results-reach-the-run-witness
    class: inject
    when:
      write: "*.ts"
      contains-any: ["reports/", "REPORT_PATH", "writeReport", "-report.json", "-results.json"]
    dedupe-of: "genesis/a2o/schemas/sprint-report.schema.json (the run envelope, Ajv-strict-validated before every write in scripts/build-sprint-report.ts) + elohim/sdk/schemas/v1/views/verdict-view.schema.json (the per-check evidence)"
    why: >
      This file mints a result artifact, and the shape is OWNED — do not hand-roll one.
      A verification result is exactly three things and there is no fourth: one ASSERTION is a
      CheckWitness{checkId, outcome, summary, observed} (elohim/epr/src/verdict.rs); one RUN is the
      envelope {runId, gitCommit, env{}, declared{}} carrying checks[]; the MEMO of a run is a
      measure-fold@1 keyed (subject x measure@version x env).
      `check_id` IS the `@concern:` tag IS the `checks:` string in genesis/manifests/habits.yaml —
      that one identifier is the only join between this artifact, the register, and CI. Reuse it.
      OUTCOME IS THREE-VALUED AND CLOSED: passed | failed | skipped. There is no `warn`. An
      unreachable probe is `skipped` with its reason in `summary`; a measured negative is `failed`
      and the decision to keep it non-blocking stays at the gate; genuinely undecidable is
      Decision::Refer{ContestedEvidence}. An assertion that never ran is ABSENT from checks[] and
      surfaces in declared.notMeasured — absence is DERIVED against a denominator declared outside
      the run, never emitted by the runner that failed.
      IF THE BYTES COME FROM A TOOL WE DO NOT OWN (cucumber-js, cargo test, clippy, junit): that is
      a FOREIGN measure. Do not conform the tool and do not write a new schema for it — declare a
      row in .claude/epr-meta/measures.yaml (family, procedure naming the external producer, unit
      naming its shape) and translate at the point of READ. Variety belongs at the edge; the core
      stays narrow.
      Reasoning: genesis/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-verification-as-memoized-derivation-guidestar.md
      (§2 the classification, §5 what is refused).
    retire-when: >
      when every writer in this lane obtains the envelope from a shared writer in
      genesis/a2o/scripts/lib/ rather than constructing an object literal — at that point the shape
      is reached by construction and an advisory pointing at it has nothing left to add
---
# genesis/a2o — acceptance-to-outcome testing

Executable BDD scenarios that verify deployed artifacts match genesis intent.

## What lives here

- `features/` — Gherkin feature files organised by domain (`lamad/`, `auth/`, `dataplane/`, …)
- `steps/` — Cucumber step-definition TypeScript
- `src/framework/` — world, fixtures, page-models, substrate-scope gate
- `scripts/` — sprint-report aggregator, coverage scanner, `look`/`graphos` tooling
- `schemas/` — JSON schema for the sprint-report wire format

## Conventions and the story review gate

Feature authoring conventions are documented in `genesis/a2o/CLAUDE.md` — tag requirements
(`@e2e`, `@concern:`, `@requires:`), persona patterns, step-definition paths, and the story-first
workflow. The `a2o-story-blind-reader-review` rule is deliberately semantic: after an authoring pass,
a fresh agent receives only the feature file and reports whether an unfamiliar reader can reconstruct
who benefits, why the behavior matters, and how each scenario proves that value. The companion
PostToolUse signal routes the author to the general `blind-reader` with its `a2o-story` profile;
findings feed a revise-and-reread loop.

## Governance note

This manifest records considered ownership (`covers: subtree`). The blind-reader rule protects story
interpretability; `new-feature-subdir-needs-meta` prevents orphan subtrees.
